WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.831
Welcome to Module Eight: Model Deployment.

2
00:00:03.161 --> 00:00:06.781
Here, your fine-tuned model becomes a real production API.

3
00:00:07.581 --> 00:00:11.008
Picture a small team whose model works beautifully in a notebook.

4
00:00:11.338 --> 00:00:15.512
Then sales signs three customers, and each wants an endpoint by Friday.

5
00:00:16.312 --> 00:00:19.953
The first attempt is a Python script on one GPU server.

6
00:00:20.283 --> 00:00:23.945
It falls over under load, and nobody knows how fast it really is.

7
00:00:24.745 --> 00:00:30.263
Lesson one serves the model with vLLM, behind an OpenAI-compatible API.

8
00:00:30.593 --> 00:00:33.785
You'll call it with curl and Python, just like a hosted model.

9
00:00:34.585 --> 00:00:36.882
Each customer has its own LoRA adapter.

10
00:00:37.212 --> 00:00:41.130
Merge one into the weights for speed, or serve many on one shared base.

11
00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:46.403
You'll also run NVIDIA NIM, a container with an optimized engine inside.

12
00:00:46.733 --> 00:00:50.587
And you'll lay out a Triton model repository with dynamic batching.

13
00:00:51.387 --> 00:00:54.238
Lesson two makes it reproducible with containers.

14
00:00:54.568 --> 00:00:58.691
The NVIDIA Container Toolkit passes GPUs into Docker,

15
00:00:58.741 --> 00:01:01.131
and NGC supplies tested images.

16
00:01:01.931 --> 00:01:06.084
On Kubernetes, the GPU Operator makes GPUs schedulable.

17
00:01:06.414 --> 00:01:10.993
Pods request GPUs, pass health probes, and load weights from a shared cache.

18
00:01:11.793 --> 00:01:16.266
Small models can share one GPU with MIG slices or time-slicing.

19
00:01:16.596 --> 00:01:19.426
Each gets its own piece, and the hardware stays busy.

20
00:01:20.226 --> 00:01:23.163
Scaling is where LLMs break the usual rules.

21
00:01:23.493 --> 00:01:28.712
CPU usage says little, so you'll autoscale on queue depth or KV cache usage.

22
00:01:29.512 --> 00:01:31.681
Lesson three asks: how fast is it?

23
00:01:32.011 --> 00:01:34.691
For a language model, there are at least four answers.

24
00:01:35.491 --> 00:01:40.561
Time to first token is responsiveness, and time per output token is streaming speed.

25
00:01:40.891 --> 00:01:45.279
Throughput counts all tokens, and goodput counts only those that met your target.

26
00:01:46.079 --> 00:01:50.103
As concurrency rises, throughput climbs while each user waits longer.

27
00:01:50.433 --> 00:01:52.623
You'll measure that curve with GenAI-Perf.

28
00:01:53.423 --> 00:01:56.211
Then Little's law turns the measurement into a fleet size.

29
00:01:56.541 --> 00:02:00.907
From a traffic forecast and a latency target, you'll count the GPUs you need.

30
00:02:01.707 --> 00:02:05.646
For our team, Friday arrives with three endpoints on one shared cluster.

31
00:02:05.976 --> 00:02:09.019
Each scales on its own, with a latency it can promise.

32
00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:15.060
You'll leave able to serve, containerize, scale, and size a real LLM deployment.

33
00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:18.796
Lesson one puts your model behind an API, so let's serve it.
